Task 1: Set Up and Connect to a Remote System Using SSH
Subtask 1.1: Verify SSH Installation
Check if the SSH client is installed on your local machine:

ssh -V
Outcome: You should see the SSH version (e.g., OpenSSH_8.9p1).
Troubleshooting: If not installed, install it using:
Ubuntu/Debian:
sudo apt update && sudo apt install openssh-client -y
CentOS/RHEL/Fedora:
sudo dnf install openssh-clients -y
Ensure the SSH server is running on the remote machine:

sudo systemctl status sshd
Outcome: The service should be active (running).
Troubleshooting: If not running, start it with:
sudo systemctl start sshd && sudo systemctl enable sshd
Subtask 1.2: Connect to the Remote Server
Use the following command to connect:

ssh username@remote_server_ip
Replace username with your remote user and remote_server_ip with the server’s IP.
Outcome: You will be prompted for the remote user’s password.
After successful authentication, you should see the remote server’s command prompt.

Task 2: Implement SSH Key-Based Authentication
Subtask 2.1: Generate SSH Key Pair
On your local machine, generate an SSH key pair:
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "your_email@example.com"
Explanation:
-t ed25519: Uses the Ed25519 algorithm (modern and secure).
-C: Adds a comment (optional).
Press Enter to accept the default key location (~/.ssh/id_ed25519).
Outcome: A public (id_ed25519.pub) and private (id_ed25519) key pair is created.
Subtask 2.2: Copy the Public Key to the Remote Server
Use ssh-copy-id to transfer the public key:

ssh-copy-id -i ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub username@remote_server_ip
Outcome: The public key is added to ~/.ssh/authorized_keys on the remote server.
Verify key-based authentication:

ssh username@remote_server_ip
Expected Result: You should log in without a password.
Task 3: Securely Transfer Files Using scp
Subtask 3.1: Copy a File from Local to Remote
Transfer a local file (localfile.txt) to the remote server:
scp localfile.txt username@remote_server_ip:/remote/directory/
Outcome: The file is securely copied to the remote directory.
Subtask 3.2: Copy a File from Remote to Local
Retrieve a file (remotefile.txt) from the remote server:
scp username@remote_server_ip:/remote/directory/remotefile.txt ~/local/directory/
Outcome: The file is downloaded to your local machine.
